{"prompt": "I failed my driving test for the third time and I feel like an idiot.", "response": "Three attempts doesn't make you an idiot — it makes you someone who keeps showing up, which is genuinely hard. Plenty of great drivers needed several tries; the test measures nerves as much as skill. Be gentle with yourself today. When you're ready, ask your examiner's feedback sheet what specifically to practice, and take a lesson focused just on that."}
{"prompt": "My cat died last week and I can't stop feeling guilty that I didn't notice she was sick sooner.", "response": "I'm so sorry. That guilt is a sign of how much you loved her — but cats are famously good at hiding illness; even vets often catch things late. You gave her a home where she felt safe her whole life. Grief takes the time it takes, and it's okay to miss her out loud."}
{"prompt": "I applied for my dream job and I think I bombed the interview. My hands were shaking the whole time.", "response": "First, take a deep breath. Your nerves don't mean you did poorly — they mean you genuinely wanted this, and that courage to show up matters more than perfection. Interviewers are used to nervous candidates; they care far more about whether you connected with them and understood the role. Whatever happens, you practiced a skill that gets better every time. And if it doesn't work out, the right opportunity will appreciate you exactly as you are."}
{"prompt": "I've been trying to learn to code for six months but I still feel like I don't understand anything. Everyone else seems to get it so fast.", "response": "What you're feeling is incredibly common — it has a name: the \"valley of despair.\" Every expert was once where you are right now. The reason others \"seem\" to get it fast is that you only see their finished work, not their hours of confusion. Six months is actually great progress; you've built more resilience than you realize. Try celebrating tiny wins — one function that works, one bug you fixed. You're not behind. You're exactly where you need to be."}
{"prompt": "I'm in a new city and I have no friends here. Every group chat invitation feels like torture.", "response": "Starting over in a new place can feel incredibly lonely, but you're not alone in feeling this way — lots of people feel like everyone else already has their \"group\" while they're still figuring things out. It's okay to start small: one coffee with one person, one local class or meetup about something you genuinely enjoy. Friendships at any age take time to build, and the fact that you're reaching out, even just to vent about it here, shows real courage. This chapter will get better."}
{"prompt": "My boss gave my idea credit to someone else in front of the whole team. I'm so angry but I don't know what to do.", "response": "That's a really hurtful experience, and your anger is completely valid. Before you react, take a moment to breathe and think about what outcome would feel most fair to you. Sometimes a calm, private conversation with your boss — something like, \"I noticed my idea from Monday's meeting was presented as John's — I'd love to make sure my contributions are visible going forward\" — can work wonders. You deserve to be recognized, and advocating for yourself is a skill worth practicing, even when it's uncomfortable."}
{"prompt": "I keep comparing myself to my sister. She's so successful and I feel like I'm a disappointment to everyone, especially my parents.", "response": "Comparison is such a thief of joy, and it sounds like you've been carrying this weight for a long time. Here's something worth remembering: your sister's path is not a measuring stick for yours. You have different strengths, different timing, and different dreams — none of which are invalid. Your parents love you for who you are, not for how you stack up to anyone else. If it helps, try writing down three things about yourself that you're proud of, completely unrelated to your sister. You matter for your own story, not as a supporting character in someone else's."}
